ions-law-budget/institutions-and-bodies/search-all-eu-institutions-and-bodies/european-parliament_en","support_summary":"The European Parliament is directly elected by EU voters.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}},{"file":"V100 institutional reference check","verificationUrl":"https://www.europarl.europa.eu/about-parliament/en/organisation-and-rules/organisation/members","checkedAt":"2026-10-09","supportScope":"Direct universal suffrage"}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":165,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"European Parliament electoral legitimacy","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B02:european-parliament-electoral-legitimacy","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:european-parliament-electoral-legitimacy"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"supporting_text_records_available","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"986344ddf1df384698f93fab2dfe4d18824afd887a47be14e45a34f115e3fc03","fingerprint":"2e00747d0006dd50b90e","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"supporting_text_records_available","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B02-20261007-167","qid":950167,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-167","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-167"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"History & Politics","group":"Fiscal versus monetary policy","stem":"Which measure is an example of fiscal rather than monetary policy?","choices":["A central bank changing banks’ reserve requirements","A central bank changing its policy interest rate","A government increasing spending on public schools","A central bank buying bonds for monetary-policy purposes","A central bank changing its lending terms to commercial banks"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"A government increasing spending on public schools","explanation":"Fiscal policy concerns government taxation and spending. Monetary policy concerns money and financial conditions and is normally conducted by a central bank.","format":"classification","references":[{"file":"IMF — Fiscal Policy: Taking and Giving Away","verificationUrl":"https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/fandd/issues/Series/Back-to-Basics/Fiscal-Policy","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-E09","title":"IMF — Fiscal Policy: Taking and Giving Away","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/Publications/fandd/issues/Series/Back-to-Basics/Fiscal-Policy","support_summary":"Fiscal policy uses government spending and taxation; central-bank policy is monetary policy.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":167,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Fiscal versus monetary policy","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B02:fiscal-versus-monetary-policy","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:fiscal-versus-monetary-policy"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"supporting_text_records_available","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"34e1720330a07fad0dd88b9bc8d88402ebd680b13263a34d07a0857bbade856e","fingerprint":"f87c819e028dc42c85d3","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"supporting_text_records_available","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B02-20261007-169","qid":950169,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-169","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-169"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"History & Politics","group":"Budget deficit versus debt stock","stem":"Which statement correctly distinguishes an annual budget deficit from public debt?","choices":["The deficit is all money ever borrowed; debt is only this year’s taxes","A deficit exists only when a government has no debt","Debt disappears automatically whenever one year has a balanced budget","Both terms mean the total value of exports","The deficit is a shortfall over a year; debt is an outstanding amount owed"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":"The deficit is a shortfall over a year; debt is an outstanding amount owed","explanation":"One concept concerns flows during a period and the other a stock at a point in time. Balancing this year’s budget does not automatically repay earlier borrowing.","format":"distinction","references":[{"file":"Merriam-Webster — Deficit","verificationUrl":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/deficit","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-E11","title":"Merriam-Webster — Deficit","url":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/deficit","support_summary":"A budget deficit is expenditure exceeding revenue.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"editorial_dictionary","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}},{"file":"Merriam-Webster — National Debt","verificationUrl":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/national%20debt","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-E12","title":"Merriam-Webster — National Debt","url":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/national%20debt","support_summary":"National debt is the total money owed by a national government.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"editorial_dictionary","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":169,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Budget deficit versus debt stock","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B02:budget-deficit-versus-debt-stock","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:budget-deficit-versus-debt-stock"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"supporting_text_records_available","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"bf7356e2906cb311e1585d9da0f17058526356d5cf1c3a0eeea7878c69565efb","fingerprint":"49a1242e59b2b4a30c4a","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"supporting_text_records_available","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B02-20261007-173","qid":950173,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-173","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-173"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Geography & Civics","group":"European mountain systems and political geography","stem":"Which pairing of mountain range and geographical setting is correct?","choices":["Pyrenees — Norway and Sweden; Apennines — Greece","Pyrenees — France and Spain; Apennines — the Italian peninsula","Pyrenees — France and Germany; Apennines — the Balkan peninsula","Pyrenees — Switzerland and Italy; Apennines — Scandinavia","Pyrenees — Italy and Austria; Apennines — the Iberian peninsula"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"Pyrenees — France and Spain; Apennines — the Italian peninsula","explanation":"The Pyrenees form a mountain barrier between France and Spain, while the Apennines run along the Italian peninsula. The pairing connects physical regions with political geography; the Alps lie farther north, across several European countries.","format":"matching","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Pyrénées–Mont Perdu","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/773/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Pyrenees","key":"Pyrenees","title":"UNESCO — Pyrénées–Mont Perdu","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/773/","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"UNESCO describes the Pyrenees/Mont Perdu landscape as spanning the France–Spain border.","retrieval_reference":"turn259132view2","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"resumed_web_read","retrieval_record":"turn315344view0"}},{"file":"UNESCO — Appennino Tosco-Emiliano Biosphere Reserve","verificationUrl":"https://www.unesco.org/en/mab/appennino-tosco-emiliano","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Apennines","key":"Apennines","title":"UNESCO — Appennino Tosco-Emiliano Biosphere Reserve","url":"https://www.unesco.org/en/mab/appennino-tosco-emiliano","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"UNESCO states that the Apennine range forms the backbone of the Italian peninsula.","retrieval_reference":"turn976976view2","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"resumed_web_read","retrieval_record":"turn315344view1"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":173,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":2,"topic74":"European mountain systems and political geography","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B02R2:european-mountain-systems-and-political-geography","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:european-mountain-systems-and-political-geography"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"supporting_text_records_available","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"496abddb399fac3b0ea15f50ee9f62b5a2064f409be04066e7392a33feb92e0c","fingerprint":"7a60733e76f9d9b895de","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"supporting_text_records_available","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B02-20261007-174","qid":950174,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-174","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-174"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Geography & Civics","group":"Oxbow lakes and river change","stem":"Which process can turn a river meander into an oxbow lake?","choices":["Corals enclose a lagoon as an island subsides","A volcano collapses and rain fills its caldera","The river cuts across the meander neck and isolates the former loop","A glacier excavates a valley that is later flooded by the sea","Wind erodes a hollow between parallel sand dunes"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"The river cuts across the meander neck and isolates the former loop","explanation":"When a river cuts through a meander neck, deposition can separate the abandoned loop from the active channel. The resulting lake records a former river course rather than volcanic or glacial excavation.","format":"context_relation","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Oxbow lake","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxbow_lake","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Oxbow","key":"Oxbow","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Oxbow lake","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxbow_lake","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"