ndean Road System","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1459/","support_summary":"The Andean road system extends through countries including Peru, Bolivia and Chile along the Andes.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":176,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Andes regional association","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B02:andes-regional-association","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:andes-regional-association"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"supporting_text_records_available","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"beb416e9803ab7397d2b044de59b3c9960775e9c26504b765a66a6fcdc549114","fingerprint":"09562c86269f88bc3467","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"supporting_text_records_available","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B02-20261007-181","qid":950181,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-181","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-181"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Geography & Civics","group":"Geothermal resource origin","stem":"What is the immediate natural source of geothermal energy?","choices":["The chemical energy of petroleum","Sunlight falling on a photovoltaic panel","Wind passing through a turbine","Heat inside Earth","Motion of ocean tides"],"correctIndex":3,"answer":"Heat inside Earth","explanation":"Geothermal energy draws on Earth’s internal heat. It is not the same process as generating power from sunlight, wind or the combustion of fossil fuels.","format":"single_best_answer","references":[{"file":"US Department of Energy — Geothermal Basics","verificationUrl":"https://www.energy.gov/hgeo/geothermal/geothermal-basics","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-G09","title":"US Department of Energy — Geothermal Basics","url":"https://www.energy.gov/hgeo/geothermal/geothermal-basics","support_summary":"Geothermal energy uses heat within Earth.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":181,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Geothermal resource origin","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B02:geothermal-resource-origin","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:geothermal-resource-origin"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"supporting_text_records_available","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"6be5f1b204af10aaa022ec314617d86c6914c5beedd3b7f49db3d123d540bd68","fingerprint":"5f99b8b571790a0b314c","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"supporting_text_records_available","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B02-20261007-182","qid":950182,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-182","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-182"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Geography & Civics","group":"Isthmus versus strait","stem":"Which description defines an isthmus rather than a strait?","choices":["A narrow water passage joining two seas","A chain of islands extending across an ocean","A narrow strip of land joining two larger land areas","A river branch that ends in an inland basin","A steep-sided inlet carved by a glacier"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"A narrow strip of land joining two larger land areas","explanation":"An isthmus is land, whereas a strait is water between land masses. Keeping the contrast clear helps explain the geographical setting of canals and shipping routes.","format":"distinction","references":[{"file":"Merriam-Webster — Isthmus","verificationUrl":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/isthmus","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-G10","title":"Merriam-Webster — Isthmus","url":"https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/isthmus","support_summary":"An isthmus is a narrow land strip connecting larger land areas, with water on each side.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"editorial_dictionary","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":182,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Isthmus versus strait","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B02:isthmus-versus-strait","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:isthmus-versus-strait"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"supporting_text_records_available","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"137e203e463b699f87ab3bb390d12b32a5760e587c56387721f271d762916401","fingerprint":"4cbe9fc5d860de28fc10","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"supporting_text_records_available","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B02-20261007-195","qid":950195,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-195","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-195"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Science & Tech","group":"Transistor role in electronics","stem":"Which function is a transistor designed to perform in an electronic circuit?","choices":["Storing charge in an electric field","Amplifying or switching electrical signals","Converting electrical energy into mechanical rotation","Opposing changes in current through a magnetic field","Providing fixed electrical resistance only"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"Amplifying or switching electrical signals","explanation":"Transistors control electrical signals and underlie modern electronic circuits. Their significance is not that they create energy, but that they can control a current using another signal.","format":"single_best_answer","references":[{"file":"Computer History Museum — Invention of the Point-Contact Transistor","verificationUrl":"https://www.computerhistory.org/siliconengine/invention-of-the-point-contact-transistor/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-S03","title":"Computer History Museum — Invention of the Point-Contact Transistor","url":"https://www.computerhistory.org/siliconengine/invention-of-the-point-contact-transistor/","support_summary":"Transistors developed as semiconductor devices for amplification and switching, replacing functions of vacuum tubes and electromechanical switches.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":195,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Transistor role in electronics","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B02:transistor-role-in-electronics","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:transistor-role-in-electronics"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"supporting_text_records_available","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"eee427b83ba3f02de92d3a8914cb5f9d65c3746d21fae590a5e3bc513af7127f","fingerprint":"9ca4e03476b198db926a","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"supporting_text_records_available","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B02-20261007-196","qid":950196,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-196","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-196"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Science & Tech","group":"Fossil versus nuclear fuel","stem":"Which of these is NOT a fossil fuel?","choices":["Crude oil","Uranium","Natural gas","Lignite","Coal"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"Uranium","explanation":"Uranium supplies nuclear energy, whereas the other choices are fossil fuels or, in the case of lignite, a type of coal. Nonrenewable does not mean the same thing as fossil.","format":"exception_discrimination","references":[{"file":"US EIA — Sources of energy","verificationUrl":"https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/sources-of-energy.php","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-S04","title":"US EIA — Sources of energy","url":"https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/what-is-energy/sources-of-energy.php","support_summary":"Petroleum, natural gas and coal are fossil fuels; uranium is a nonrenewable nuclear fuel, not a fossil fuel.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":196,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Fossil versus nuclear fuel","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B02:fossil-versus-nuclear-fuel","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:fossil-versus-nuclear-fuel"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"supporting_text_records_available","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"8c2669eca3a1c7eedd37188e3ab37df3611e589033ac7e99bf6a4f11b8f2bf6f","fingerprint":"02070b09976083f64be7","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"supporting_text_records_available","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B02-20261007-197","qid":950197,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-197","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-197"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Science & Tech","group":"Main gas in Earth atmosphere","stem":"Which gas makes up the largest share of Earth’s atmosphere near the surface?","choices":["Hydrogen","Nitrogen","Carbon dioxide","Helium","Oxygen"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"Nitrogen","explanation":"Nitrogen makes up about 78% of dry air, while oxygen accounts for about 21%. A gas can have important biological or climatic effects without being the most abundant.","format":"single_best_answer","references":[{"file":"NASA — Earth Facts: Atmosphere","verificationUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-S05","title":"NASA — Earth Facts: Atmosphere","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/facts/","support_summary":"Near the surface, Earth’s atmosphere consists of about 78% nitrogen and 21% oxygen.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":197,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Main gas in Earth atmosphere","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B02:main-gas-earth-atmosphere","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:main-gas-in-earth-atmosphere"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"supporting_text_records_available","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"3e97a271383a4455cd97d4ef5977a6ea8cd4fab76b9cdfb3f34c98ec05c8bc8c","fingerprint":"f298c0c2cf526b26ff03","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"supporting_text_records_available","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B02-20261007-198","qid":950198,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-198","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-198"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Science & Tech","group":"Moon synchronous rotation","stem":"Why do observers on Earth see approximately the same face of the Moon?","choices":["Earth permanently blocks sunlight from its far side","The Moon stays at one fixed point in space","Its rotation period matches its orbital period around Earth","It completes one rotation every Earth day","It does not rotate at all"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Its rotation period matches its orbital period around Earth","explanation":"The Moon does rotate. Turning once during each orbit keeps roughly the same hemisphere facing Earth; the far side is not permanently dark.","format":"single_best_answer","references":[{"file":"NASA — Moon Facts: Orbit and Rotation","verificationUrl":"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-S06","title":"NASA — Moon Facts: Orbit and Rotation","url":"https://science.nasa.gov/moon/facts/","support_summary":"The Moon rotates once in the same time that it completes one orbit around Earth, explaining why roughly the same face is visible.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":198,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Moon synchronous rotation","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B02:moon-synchronous-rotation","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:moon-synchronous-rotation"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"supporting_text_records_available","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"deca03c8ed05e98ac322244c2876361d06b3c0177529aa588f6e2f1265c9fe66","fingerprint":"de95411cde8d50fc215c","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"supporting_text_records_available","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B02-20261007-199","qid":950199,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-199","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B02-20261007-199"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Design","group":"Art Deco interwar visual vocabulary","stem":"Which design movement is strongly associated with interwar stylized geometry and zigzag ornament?","choices":["Deconstructivism","Gothic Revival","Art Deco","Rococo","Brutalism"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Art Deco","explanation":"Art Deco drew on modern visual forms while also making decorative and luxury objects. Its geometric vocabulary is a useful clue, not a rule that every work must display a zigzag.","format":"single_best_answer","references":[{"file":"Victoria and Albert Museum — An introduction to Art Deco","verificationUrl":"https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/an-introduction-to-art-deco","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B02-D01","title":"Victoria and Albert Museum — An introduction to Art Deco","url":"https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/an-introduction-to-art-deco","support_summary":"Art Deco developed decorative forms involving stylized geometry and zigzags; the 1925 Paris exhibition was a major showcase.","verification_status":"supporting_web_text_read","checked_on":"2026-10-07","verification_method":"Author read the cited supporting web text; not independent expert certification.","source_type":"institutional","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"previous_batch_inherited"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":199,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Art Deco interwar visual vocabulary","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B02:art-deco-interwar-visual-vocabulary","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:art-deco-interwar-visual-vocabulary"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"supporting_text_records_available","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"fe78158cc4e58eb4b8fe502d2b45bad2d2dd66e00fb78c191a695c5e4ae0b99e","fingerprint":"b6b6f3ecab2352d8f2d3","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"supporting_text_records_available","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B03-20261007-201","qid":950201,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-201","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-201"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Classical capital comparison","stem":"Which pair correctly identifies the ornament of the two classical orders?","choices":["Ionic — acanthus leaves; Corinthian — triglyphs","Ionic — coffers; Corinthian — battlements","Ionic — volutes; Corinthian — acanthus leaves","Ionic — pointed arches; Corinthian — volutes","Ionic — triglyphs; Corinthian — pointed arches"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Ionic — volutes; Corinthian — acanthus leaves","explanation":"Volutes are the scrolls of an Ionic capital; stylized acanthus leaves characterize a Corinthian capital. Triglyphs belong to the Doric frieze, not a capital.","format":"matching","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Ancient Greek architecture","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Greek_architecture","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Greek_architecture","key":"Greek_architecture","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Ancient Greek architecture","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ancient_Greek_architecture","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":201,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Classical capital comparison","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B03:classical-capital-comparison","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:classical-capital-comparison"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"reference_only_pending_external_check","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"efc3f51dbd10fe3ab60ab93dabb9deea28ee984f4f2c326b64f92a6b217fbc49","fingerprint":"54eda618540243c35a71","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"reference_only_pending_external_check","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B03-20261007-202","qid":950202,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-202","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-202"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Parthenon and Pantheon civilizations","stem":"Which pairing distinguishes two similarly named ancient monuments?","choices":["Parthenon — classical Athens; Pantheon — pharaonic Thebes","Parthenon — classical Athens; Pantheon — imperial Rome","Parthenon — Byzantine Constantinople; Pantheon — imperial Rome","Parthenon — Hellenistic Alexandria; Pantheon — medieval Florence","Parthenon — imperial Rome; Pantheon — classical Athens"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"Parthenon — classical Athens; Pantheon — imperial Rome","explanation":"The Parthenon is a fifth-century-BCE Athenian temple. The standing Pantheon belongs chiefly to Hadrian's second-century-CE rebuilding in Rome.","format":"matching","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Acropolis, Athens","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/404/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Acropolis","key":"Acropolis","title":"UNESCO — Acropolis, Athens","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/404/","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"Pericles rebuilding after the Persian Wars; fifth-century monuments including the Parthenon.","retrieval_reference":"turn924909view0","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Pantheon, Rome","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pantheon,_Rome","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Pantheon","key":"Pantheon","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Pantheon, Rome","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pantheon,_Rome","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":202,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Parthenon and Pantheon civilizations","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B03:parthenon-and-pantheon-civilizations","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:parthenon-and-pantheon-civilizations"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"partial_supporting_records_available","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"fd077329b1725193f6a0f161934497ca6f48ac64c99b733b76ea02604b1793af","fingerprint":"73e2240bfbd6ab27e967","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"partial_supporting_records_available","decision":"adopted"},"imageId":"PHOTO100-t63-63e0c2fa39a3","imageDependency":"helpful","mediaReview100":"exact source article for the work"},{"id":"ARCHED-B03-20261007-205","qid":950205,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-205","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-205"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"Roman spectacle buildings","stem":"Which Roman building is correctly matched with its main ancient use?","choices":["Baths of Caracalla — chariot racing","Colosseum — public bathing","Circus Maximus — chariot racing","Basilica of Maxentius — naval harbour","Pantheon — gladiatorial combat"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"Circus Maximus — chariot racing","explanation":"The circus provided a long racing course; an amphitheatre enclosed an arena. Roman baths and basilicas served different social and civic functions.","format":"matching","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Circus Maximus","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circus_Maximus","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Circus_Maximus","key":"Circus_Maximus","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Circus Maximus","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circus_Maximus","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Colosseum","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colosseum","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Colosseum","key":"Colosseum","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Colosseum","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colosseum","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":205,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Roman spectacle buildings","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B03:roman-spectacle-buildings","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:roman-spectacle-buildings"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"reference_only_pending_external_check","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"73e04c916bd69d29165d9bbad838b8e98d6640cee183c33b869234da5632b535","fingerprint":"5728780923fe010a0fae","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"reference_only_pending_external_check","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B03-20261007-209","qid":950209,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-209","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-209"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Architecture","group":"San Vitale and imperial representation","stem":"Justinian and Theodora appear in Ravenna's San Vitale mosaics because the city was linked to which power?","choices":["The Ottoman Empire","Napoleon's French Empire","The Byzantine Empire","The Carolingian Empire","The kingdom of Louis XIV"],"correctIndex":2,"answer":"The Byzantine Empire","explanation":"The sixth-century mosaics place imperial figures within a Christian sacred setting. Ravenna was an important centre of Byzantine authority in Italy.","format":"context_relation","references":[{"file":"UNESCO — Early Christian Monuments of Ravenna","verificationUrl":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/788/","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Ravenna","key":"Ravenna","title":"UNESCO — Early Christian Monuments of Ravenna","url":"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/788/","source_type":"institutional_primary","verification_status":"supporting_text_read","checked_at":"2026-10-07","support_scope":"Ravenna was a Roman and later Byzantine centre, with important fifth- and sixth-century Christian monuments and mosaics.","quoted_text":false,"retrieval_record":"turn896906view1","verification_record_origin":"saved_pre_interruption_work"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Basilica of San Vitale","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basilica_of_San_Vitale","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-San_Vitale","key":"San_Vitale","title":"Encyclopaedia r