y is distinct from Surrealism's later formal organization.","format":"context_relation","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Dada","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dada","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Dada","key":"Dada","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Dada","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dada","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":306,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Dada and wartime dissent","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B03:dada-and-wartime-dissent","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:dada-and-wartime-dissent"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"reference_only_pending_external_check","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"3f444fe761a13f1e1a2fd87a6f76af8cfd14dcda68c6ce52e4dc669f8c04bf0c","fingerprint":"fa7e4244ed59363dfb95","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"reference_only_pending_external_check","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B03-20261007-307","qid":950307,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-307","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-307"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Art History","group":"Cubist collage and phase","stem":"Which association is conventional in accounts of Cubism?","choices":["Analytic Cubism — optical colour dots; Synthetic Cubism — smooth naturalism","Analytic Cubism — fragmented analysis; Synthetic Cubism — constructed forms and collage","Analytic Cubism — collage construction; Synthetic Cubism — fragmented analysis","Analytic Cubism — expressive pure colour; Synthetic Cubism — automatic writing","Analytic Cubism — atmospheric landscape; Synthetic Cubism — academic perspective"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"Analytic Cubism — fragmented analysis; Synthetic Cubism — constructed forms and collage","explanation":"The labels distinguish tendencies within Cubism. Collage and papiers collés are especially associated with the Synthetic phase, though the boundary is not absolute.","format":"matching","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Cubism","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubism","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Cubism","key":"Cubism","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Cubism","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cubism","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":307,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Cubist collage and phase","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B03:cubist-collage-and-phase","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:cubist-collage-and-phase"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"reference_only_pending_external_check","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"4b37e16d5839aa14176d706334d107cb596743759d5971b55cdff68e56ba5c31","fingerprint":"90d98091ecc97d390754","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"reference_only_pending_external_check","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B03-20261007-308","qid":950308,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-308","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-308"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":["HUB-BAROQUE-SCULPTURE","ART93-t63-45fe2fa7a56d"],"major":"Art History","group":"Renaissance versus Baroque sculpture","stem":"Which association correctly distinguishes two sculptures of David?","choices":["Michelangelo — poised marble figure; Bernini — twisting figure in action","Michelangelo — twisting marble figure; Bernini — poised bronze figure","Michelangelo — poised bronze figure; Bernini — twisting wooden figure","Michelangelo — seated marble figure; Bernini — standing bronze figure","Michelangelo — reclining bronze figure; Bernini — seated marble figure"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Michelangelo — poised marble figure; Bernini — twisting figure in action","explanation":"Michelangelo's David presents a poised figure, while Bernini's later David twists into the action of throwing. The same subject can express different period styles.","format":"matching","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — David (Michelangelo)","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_(Michelangelo)","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Michelangelo_David","key":"Michelangelo_David","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — David (Michelangelo)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_(Michelangelo)","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — David (Bernini)","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_(Bernini)","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Bernini_David","key":"Bernini_David","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — David (Bernini)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_(Bernini)","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":308,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Renaissance versus Baroque sculpture","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B03:renaissance-versus-baroque-sculpture","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:renaissance-versus-baroque-sculpture"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"reference_only_pending_external_check","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"6f48f8e6536e8f6c536118b5a3a3a4b0ff607841ad3badaffaf3cb5f07f926ee","fingerprint":"56a352b6c6560886e8f4","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"reference_only_pending_external_check","decision":"adopted"},"imageId":"ART93-t63-45fe2fa7a56d","imageDependency":"helpful","mediaReview100":"exact source article for the work"},{"id":"ARCHED-B03-20261007-309","qid":950309,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-309","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-309"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Art History","group":"Christian and classical subjects","stem":"Which subject comes from classical mythology rather than the Bible?","choices":["Judith and Holofernes","The judgment of Paris","The sacrifice of Isaac","The marriage at Cana","The calling of Saint Matthew"],"correctIndex":1,"answer":"The judgment of Paris","explanation":"Paris's judgment concerns the goddesses Hera, Athena and Aphrodite. The other subjects belong to biblical narratives frequently represented in European art.","format":"exception_discrimination","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Judgement of Paris","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judgement_of_Paris","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Judgment_Paris","key":"Judgment_Paris","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Judgement of Paris","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judgement_of_Paris","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":309,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Christian and classical subjects","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B03:christian-and-classical-subjects","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:christian-and-classical-subjects"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"reference_only_pending_external_check","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"0c4699c457514dcfbfc81b0a204f037a9bf9249545d998233df2616fb3b60900","fingerprint":"5f73cda13b8f16f0d05e","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"reference_only_pending_external_check","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B03-20261007-311","qid":950311,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-311","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-311"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":[],"major":"Art History","group":"Printmaking processes contrast","stem":"Which pairing distinguishes relief from intaglio printing?","choices":["Woodcut — raised surface prints; engraving — recessed lines print","Woodcut — photographic exposure; engraving — flat chemically treated stone","Woodcut — recessed lines print; engraving — raised surface prints","Woodcut — flat chemically treated stone; engraving — raised surface prints","Woodcut — stencil openings; engraving — photographic exposure"],"correctIndex":0,"answer":"Woodcut — raised surface prints; engraving — recessed lines print","explanation":"A woodcut is a relief process. Engraving is intaglio, where ink in incised lines transfers to paper. The words describe processes, not just visual styles.","format":"matching","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Printmaking","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Printmaking","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Printmaking","key":"Printmaking","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Printmaking","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Printmaking","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}}],"examLanguages":["en","it"],"scope":"shared","version":"editorial-review-v100","editorialStatus74":"ready","reviewStatus":"editorially_reviewed","publication_ready":true,"previewOnly95":false,"seriesNumber95":311,"canonical_number":null,"revision95":1,"topic74":"Printmaking processes contrast","skill74":"context_relation","testedRelation95":"B03:printmaking-processes-contrast","practiceRepetitionKeys95":["TOPIC:printmaking-processes-contrast"],"workIds95":[],"evidenceStatus95":"reference_only_pending_external_check","exactDuplicateOf95":[],"sourceSnapshotHash95":"976e704fb6f627248bb51d92798d8fe8e96b89b2a7b294a423eab66afb1b23c8","fingerprint":"a9b7bb88cb1ccaf7fb5b","review100":{"date":"2026-10-09","scope":"Stem, five alternatives, correct answer and explanation reviewed. Retained source provenance is not an assertion of fresh external verification.","sourceVerification":"reference_only_pending_external_check","decision":"adopted"}},{"id":"ARCHED-B03-20261007-312","qid":950312,"nodeId":"REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-312","relatedNodeIds":["REVIEW95-ARCHED-B03-20261007-312"],"sourceAtlasNodeIds":["ALPHA-B004-Q354"],"major":"Art History","group":"Art movement geography","stem":"Which pair correctly associates a movement with an important early centre?","choices":["Fauvism — Berlin; De Stijl — Austria","Fauvism — Milan; De Stijl — Russia","Fauvism — London; De Stijl — Germany","Fauvism — Vienna; De Stijl — France","Fauvism — Paris; De Stijl — the Netherlands"],"correctIndex":4,"answer":"Fauvism — Paris; De Stijl — the Netherlands","explanation":"Fauvism emerged in the Parisian context of the early 1900s. De Stijl was organized in the Netherlands around 1917 and pursued a different abstract vocabulary.","format":"matching","references":[{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — Fauvism","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fauvism","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-Fauvism","key":"Fauvism","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — Fauvism","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fauvism","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","quoted_text":false,"verification_record_origin":"reference_only"}},{"file":"Encyclopaedia reference — De Stijl","verificationUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Stijl","sourceMetadata95":{"id":"B03-S-De_Stijl","key":"De_Stijl","title":"Encyclopaedia reference — De Stijl","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_Stijl","source_type":"tertiary_encyclopaedia","verification_status":"reference_locator_not_retrieved_this_pass","checked_at":null,"support_scope":"Reference supplied for this topic; no claim that its live text was read in this pass.","